"bytes\": 209,\n+      \"sha256\": \"ffecb0f74522bea4f6a97bd3e564a811848541d53b484cd8e760c7537a550272\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"path\": \"TURN_5_LOG.md\",\n+      \"bytes\": 1083,\n+      \"sha256\": \"143ba95256d2a3917713dd0e5e8488bcd025e5ce3448e6d04907b8226baf43ce\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"path\": \"TURN_5_MANIFEST.json\",\n+      \"bytes\": 5807,\n+      \"sha256\": \"54f1ce2c5c3fbc114b3919abe99bcd01370b0bd4eb1560815f4f6f4ef8a5b5a0\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"path\": \"verify_turn1.py\",\n+      \"bytes\": 2420,\n+      \"sha256\": \"772c6ffb4a5a931b4765b524e326b57fc49c80710835de0a19459842d5a78f4f\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"path\": \"verify_turn2.py\",\n+      \"bytes\": 1791,\n+      \"sha256\": \"36948aac534ddca2c72448419467c237566d51114bb60759e7b39b1828d589fd\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"path\": \"verify_turn3.py\",\n+      \"bytes\": 1293,\n+      \"sha256\": \"8bbc20bd318a9c8901eaa64c8ca316a012dc80b408abb5f3db935338099d19dc\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"path\": \"verify_turn4.py\",\n+      \"bytes\": 1321,\n+      \"sha256\": \"f15ef162bc0bc2889432adcff447434d74321eef40ed019fa19e15d1ff09fa4e\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"path\": \"verify_turn5.py\",\n+      \"bytes\": 1728,\n+      \"sha256\": \"35587968988f847c7e066eb028398182ba9cf30ecea5860de23050037336fd9d\"\n+    }\n+  ]\n+}"},{"sha":"73fa1397ebc018d348b5fb638b35fa36f6f1dbe4","filename":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/2302055/FINAL_README.md","status":"added","additions":15,"deletions":0,"changes":15,"blob_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/blob/d9e4600d05b8272fe913a22ae7dcc0fbe0a26344/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2302055%2FFINAL_README.md","raw_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/raw/d9e4600d05b8272fe913a22ae7dcc0fbe0a26344/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2302055%2FFINAL_README.md","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/contents/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2302055%2FFINAL_README.md?ref=d9e4600d05b8272fe913a22ae7dcc0fbe0a26344","patch":"@@ -0,0 +1,15 @@\n+# Final five-turn packet: 2302055 / AMR-022-2055\n+\n+Start with RESULT.md and SOURCE_SCOPE.md. The original three-arbitrary-entire-input problem is unresolved, 5/5. The main explicit theorem permits one R(exp(Q)) input and two arbitrary inputs. All classical dependencies are credited; the original full Rubel–Squires–Taylor proof was not retrieved, although its statement was checked in Demailly's primary text.\n+\n+Read TURN_1.md through TURN_5.md for the complete scoped arguments. Historical source and turn manifests remain unchanged. FINAL_AUTHOR_MANIFEST.json binds all other public files. Primary PDFs are retained separately and identified by SOURCE_MANIFEST.json and SOURCE_ADDITION_T4.json; no raw PDFs or imported database records are included in this public packet.\n+\n+Run, with Python's standard library:\n+\n+    python verify_turn1.py\n+    python verify_turn2.py\n+    python verify_turn3.py\n+    python verify_turn4.py\n+    python verify_turn5.py\n+\n+Each complete stdout must equal its TURN_n_CHECKS.json byte-for-byte. The total is 38,066 exact assertions. Counts describe finite algebraic controls, not automatic proof of the analytic claims. FINAL_REVIEW_REQUEST.md specifies the full independent audit scope. No further author search is undertaken after this freeze. Independent review and a publication gate are still required."},{"sha":"8bacd5d1de19346af470c7be053e877030f70d58","filename":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/2302055/FINAL_REVIEW_REQUEST.md","status":"added","additions":18,"deletions":0,"changes":18,"blob_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/blob/d9e4600d05b8272fe913a22ae7dcc0fbe0a26344/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2302055%2FFINAL_REVIEW_REQUEST.md","raw_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/raw/d9e4600d05b8272fe913a22ae7dcc0fbe0a26344/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2302055%2FFINAL_REVIEW_REQUEST.md","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/contents/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2302055%2FFINAL_REVIEW_REQUEST.md?ref=d9e4600d05b8272fe913a22ae7dcc0fbe0a26344","patch":"@@ -0,0 +1,18 @@\n+# Full independent review request: 2302055\n+\n+## Requested disposition and exact scope\n+\n+Review all five substantive turns, the primary source comparison and the sharp gaps. Proposed original disposition is **unsolved, 5/5**. The one-input positive theorem is a scoped result; neither the monodromy barrier nor the absence of character eigenfunctions is an original counterexample. No novelty certification is requested.\n+\n+## High-risk checks\n+\n+1. Verify the complete Hayman–Lingham Problem 2.55 and update (printed43/PDF44), Demailly's printed179–180 and complete C.R. note39–40. Credit the already-known polynomial-input and two-simple-exponential cases. Retain the exact caveat that the original full Rubel–Squires–Taylor proof remains unretrieved; Demailly explicitly states the irreducibility theorem used.\n+2. Turn1: the entire matrix functional calculus and characteristic polynomial must include all critical fibers with algebraic multiplicity, retain ambient-entire coefficients, and use connectedness only with its stated dependency. Check that polynomial precomposition is a two-way equivalence, and that the torus quotient is an actual connected regular cover after singular-locus removal.\n+3. Turn2: verify the disjoint branch-puncture monodromy proof, the rank-two winding lattice argument, degree bounds and exceptional constant coefficient. Check the local sphere isotopies and their lifted extensions at branch points and punctures. The finite component count must be locally constant before symmetric coefficients are formed; exceptional fibers are covered only by the explicit density argument.\n+4. Turn3: audit the finite-fiber plurisubharmonic maximum, bounded extension across the analytic base subset, attainment of a global maximum, and density of the regular quotient. Apply Lin's theorem only to a connected cyclic cover of an ultra-Liouville base. The displayed non-ultra-Liouville quotient is on an already-positive source example, not a counterexample.\n+5. Turn4: distinguish inverse monodromy from deck transformations of the original cover. Check the normal closure, pullback component deck subgroup, finite-index nilpotent core, surjectivity onto the original connected pullback, and final density. Verify the full Z wreath Z monodromy and universal finite-difference proof excluding virtually nilpotent subgroups. Finite-type alone is not promoted to the criterion.\n+6. Turn5: audit descent for arbitrary unitary characters, strictly subunit exponents and removability at pairwise coordinate crossings, the common-translation line and decay bound, and finite-dimensional simultaneous diagonalization. Ensure the infinite-dimensional gap is retained. The result excludes exponential-polynomial candidate functions, not arbitrary ambient entire functions.\n+\n+## Integrity and reproducibility\n+\n+Verify the final manifest, all historical manifests and source PDF hashes. Replay the five scripts and compare their full stdout exactly to the stored receipts. Expected author total is 38,066 assertions. Independently check representative algebra and topology; the supplied finite controls do not replace the analytic proofs. Provide a neutral portable review with exact author bindings and any mandatory additive corrections. Do not alter frozen author files or add a sixth author search. Publication remains gated."},{"sha":"6f448d061e35521fa3ad05679dcfc65ce1eb5749","filename":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/2302055/PRIOR_GATE.json","status":"added","additions":27,"deletions":0,"changes":27,"blob_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/blob/d9e4600d05b8272fe913a22ae7dcc0fbe0a26344/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2302055%2FPRIOR_GATE.json","raw_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/raw/d9e4600d05b8272fe913a22ae7dcc0fbe0a26344/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2302055%2FPRIOR_GATE.json","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/contents/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2302055%2FPRIOR_GATE.json?ref=d9e4600d05b8272fe913a22ae7dcc0fbe0a26344","patch":"@@ -0,0 +1,27 @@\n+{\n+  \"problem_id\": 2302055,\n+  \"alias\": \"AMR-022-2055\",\n+  \"date\": \"2026-10-02\",\n+  \"current_main\": \"efd29c05204703acca9a0860812f54b94fae54b1\",\n+  \"queue_rank\": 393,\n+  \"queue_status\": \"queued\",\n+  \"queue_turns\": \"0/5\",\n+  \"live_branches_checked\": 403,\n+  \"mirrored_refs_checked\": 443,\n+  \"exact_matching_prior_attempts_found\": 0,\n+  \"search_terms\": [\n+    \"2302055\",\n+    \"AMR-022-2055\",\n+    \"2.55 Rubel\",\n+    \"Demailly bounded\",\n+    \"Liouville\",\n+    \"irreducibility\",\n+    \"sum entire\"\n+  ],\n+  \"target_paths_checked\": [\n+    \"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/2302055\",\n+    \"attempts/2302055\"\n+  ],\n+  \"all_blob_fulltext_exhaustive\": false,\n+  \"pinned_import_revision\": \"37e53eabe540fb458758e198be61634bd02ee008\"\n+}"},{"sha":"bf7822b086241e2cb2e2703061c2f91a9a77b6bb","filename":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/2302055/PUBLICATION_MANIFEST.json","status":"added","additions":245,"deletions":0,"changes":245,"blob_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/blob/d9e4600d05b8272fe913a22ae7dcc0fbe0a26344/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2302055%2FPUBLICATION_MANIFEST.json","raw_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/raw/d9e4600d05b8272fe913a22ae7dcc0fbe0a26344/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2302055%2FPUBLICATION_MANIFEST.json","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/contents/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2302055%2FPUBLICATION_MANIFEST.json?ref=d9e4600d05b8272fe913a22ae7dcc0fbe0a26344","patch":"@@ -0,0 +1,245 @@\n+{\n+  \"problem_id\": 2302055,\n+  \"files\": [\n+    {\n+      \"path\": \"CURRENT_STATE.json\",\n+      \"bytes\": 390,\n+      \"sha256\": \"c0da802ff077dae7b8a8e658d3c1ce0b518a556